<% content_for :title, "Statuspage Alternative for Agencies | StatusPulse" %>
<% content_for :description, "StatusPulse is a Statuspage alternative for agencies: isolated client workspaces, a public /status/<client> URL each, and HTTP checks without mixing client data." %>

<aside class="mk-railstrip" aria-hidden="true" data-controller="rail">
  <span class="mk-railstrip__mark"><svg width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.4" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><polyline points="22 12 18 12 15 21 9 3 6 12 2 12"></polyline></svg></span>
  <div class="mk-railstrip__bars" data-rail-target="bars"></div>
  <span class="mk-railstrip__label">Agency middle</span>
</aside>

<div class="mk-shell">
<header class="mk-nav mk-wrap">
  <a href="<%= root_path %>" class="mk-nav__brand"><span class="mk-nav__mark"><svg width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.6" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><polyline points="22 12 18 12 15 21 9 3 6 12 2 12"></polyline></svg></span><span class="mk-nav__word">StatusPulse</span></a>
  <nav class="mk-nav__links">
    <a href="<%= uptime_monitoring_for_agencies_path %>">Agencies</a>
    <a href="<%= multi_tenant_uptime_monitoring_path %>">Workspaces</a>
    <a href="<%= client_uptime_reports_path %>">Reports</a>
    <a href="<%= public_status_path %>">Demo status</a>
    <a href="<%= login_path %>" class="mk-nav__signin">Sign in</a>
    <a href="<%= signup_path %>" class="mk-btn mk-btn--sm">Create workspace</a>
  </nav>
</header>

<main>
  <section class="mk-hero mk-wrap">
    <div class="mk-hero__copy">
      <p class="mk-eyebrow">Statuspage alternative for agencies</p>
      <h1 class="mk-h1">A Statuspage alternative for agencies.<br> <span class="mk-h1__outline">Isolated client workspaces.</span></h1>
      <p class="mk-lede">UptimeRobot is built for uptime checks in a single-tenant account. Atlassian Statuspage is built for status communication, not agency isolation. StatusPulse is the agency-shaped middle: one isolated workspace per client, real HTTP checks, and a public <code class="mk-code">/status/&lt;client&gt;</code> URL.</p>
      <div class="mk-hero__actions">
        <a href="<%= signup_path %>" class="mk-btn">Create a workspace</a>
        <a href="<%= public_status_path %>" class="mk-btn mk-btn--ghost">View status page demo</a>
      </div>
      <p class="mk-hero__note">Self-hostable. Runs on Postgres. No agent to install on client infrastructure.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="mk-console" aria-hidden="true">
      <div class="mk-console__bar"><span></span><span></span><span></span></div>
<pre class="mk-console__code">uptimerobot: one account, mixed monitors
statuspage:  status comms, not tenant isolation

statuspulse:
  northwind  /status/northwind   isolated
  meridian   /status/meridian    isolated
  kestrel    /status/kestrel     isolated</pre>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="mk-section mk-wrap">
    <div class="mk-section__head">
      <div class="mk-ticks" aria-hidden="true"><span></span><span></span><span></span><span></span><span></span><span></span><span></span><span></span><span></span><span></span></div>
      <p class="mk-eyebrow">Honest split</p>
      <h2 class="mk-h2">UptimeRobot checks. Statuspage communicates. Agencies need both, isolated.</h2>
      <p class="mk-body">Agencies that cannot mix client data outgrow a shared monitor list and a status-comms tool that is not a workspace. StatusPulse keeps checks, incidents, and the public status URL inside the same client boundary.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="mk-grid mk-grid--3">
      <article class="mk-card"><h3 class="mk-h3">UptimeRobot</h3><p class="mk-body">Good at uptime checks. A typical agency account is single-tenant: every client&rsquo;s monitors share one list, or each client becomes another login to juggle. That is not an isolated workspace.</p></article>
      <article class="mk-card"><h3 class="mk-h3">Atlassian Statuspage</h3><p class="mk-body">Good at status communication. It is not uptime monitoring for agencies and it is not built to isolate one client&rsquo;s operational data from another&rsquo;s.</p></article>
      <article class="mk-card"><h3 class="mk-h3">StatusPulse</h3><p class="mk-body">The agency-shaped middle. Isolated client workspaces, HTTP checks, an incident paper trail, SLA reports, and a public <code class="mk-code">/status/&lt;client&gt;</code> page with no shared dashboard bleed.</p></article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="mk-section mk-wrap">
    <div class="mk-section__head"><p class="mk-eyebrow">What the client sees</p><h2 class="mk-h2">A public status URL per workspace, not a mixed board.</h2></div>
    <figure class="mk-product-shot"><img src="/product/status-page.png" width="1280" height="1551" alt="Tenant-specific StatusPulse public status page listing only that workspace's services" loading="lazy"><figcaption>Actual UI with illustrative test data. Public status routes resolve one organization by slug; service and incident queries stay scoped to that organization.</figcaption></figure>
  </section>

  <section class="mk-section mk-wrap">
    <div class="mk-section__head">
      <p class="mk-eyebrow">What ships in the workspace</p>
      <h2 class="mk-h2">UptimeRobot-style checks, bound to one client.</h2>
      <p class="mk-body">If you need UptimeRobot for client workspaces — real HTTP checks without a shared monitor list — the workspace is the unit. Operators sign in to the workspace they belong to.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="mk-grid mk-grid--3">
      <article class="mk-card"><h3 class="mk-h3">Isolated workspaces</h3><p class="mk-body">Endpoints, incidents, users, reports, and maintenance windows stay scoped to one client. Nothing bleeds into another dashboard.</p></article>
      <article class="mk-card"><h3 class="mk-h3">HTTP checks</h3><p class="mk-body">GET, POST, PUT, PATCH, DELETE, HEAD, and OPTIONS with headers, request bodies, expected status codes, and response text assertions.</p></article>
      <article class="mk-card"><h3 class="mk-h3">Evidence, not a ping log</h3><p class="mk-body">Incidents keep a paper trail. Reports cover 7, 30, and 90-day uptime plus P90 latency for the workspace that owns the checks.</p></article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="mk-cta mk-wrap">
    <h2 class="mk-h2">Start with one isolated client workspace.</h2>
    <p class="mk-body">Create the workspace, point it at an endpoint, and publish <code class="mk-code">/status/&lt;client&gt;</code> when you are ready.</p>
    <a href="<%= signup_path %>" class="mk-btn mk-btn--lg">Create a workspace</a>
  </section>

  <section class="mk-section mk-wrap" id="faq">
    <div class="mk-section__head">
      <p class="mk-eyebrow">Questions</p>
      <h2 class="mk-h2">Statuspage, UptimeRobot, and the agency middle.</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="mk-grid mk-grid--2">
      <article class="mk-card"><h3 class="mk-h3">Is StatusPulse a Statuspage alternative for agencies?</h3><p class="mk-body">Yes, for agencies that need a public status URL per client plus isolated operational data. Atlassian Statuspage is status communication. StatusPulse publishes <code class="mk-code">/status/&lt;client&gt;</code> from the same workspace that runs the checks.</p></article>
      <article class="mk-card"><h3 class="mk-h3">How does StatusPulse compare with UptimeRobot?</h3><p class="mk-body">UptimeRobot is built around uptime checks in a single-tenant account. StatusPulse runs real HTTP checks too, but each client gets an isolated workspace so monitors, incidents, and status pages do not mix.</p></article>
      <article class="mk-card"><h3 class="mk-h3">Does each client get a public status URL?</h3><p class="mk-body">Yes. Every workspace publishes at <code class="mk-code">/status/&lt;client&gt;</code>. Stakeholders see that client&rsquo;s services and incidents only — no shared dashboard bleed.</p></article>
      <article class="mk-card"><h3 class="mk-h3">Can agencies self-host StatusPulse?</h3><p class="mk-body">Yes. StatusPulse is self-hostable, runs on Postgres, and does not require an agent on client infrastructure.</p></article>
    </div>
  </section>
</main>

<% content_for :head do %>
  <script type="application/ld+json">
<%= raw json_escape(JSON.pretty_generate({
  "@context" => "https://schema.org",
  "@type" => "FAQPage",
  "mainEntity" => [
    [ "Is StatusPulse a Statuspage alternative for agencies?", "Yes, for agencies that need a public status URL per client plus isolated operational data. Atlassian Statuspage is status communication. StatusPulse publishes /status/<client> from the same workspace that runs the checks." ],
    [ "How does StatusPulse compare with UptimeRobot?", "UptimeRobot is built around uptime checks in a single-tenant account. StatusPulse runs real HTTP checks too, but each client gets an isolated workspace so monitors, incidents, and status pages do not mix." ],
    [ "Does each client get a public status URL?", "Yes. Every workspace publishes at /status/<client>. Stakeholders see that client's services and incidents only — no shared dashboard bleed." ],
    [ "Can agencies self-host StatusPulse?", "Yes. StatusPulse is self-hostable, runs on Postgres, and does not require an agent on client infrastructure." ]
  ].map { |question, answer| {
    "@type" => "Question",
    "name" => question,
    "acceptedAnswer" => { "@type" => "Answer", "text" => answer }
  } }
})) %>
  </script>
<% end %>

<%= render "site_footer" %>
</div>
